            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus views Nineveh as a dual symbol: primarily representing a wicked pagan city that repents and receives God's mercy (used by Jesus as an exemplar to condemn an unrepentant generation), and secondarily as an arrogant, violent empire destined for divine destruction (as in Nahum and Zephaniah).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines Nineveh as a 'great city of the sea' representing Gentile people who repent and rise in judgment against those who reject Jesus.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"A wicked Gentile city that repents and is spared, whose people will judge unrepentant generations; also a symbol of an oppressive empire under God's judgment.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Gentile people who repent and judge those who reject Jesus.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book focuses exclusively on the New Testament application of the Ninevites in Matthew 12:41, omitting the Old Testament prophetic tradition (Nahum/Zephaniah) of Nineveh as an arrogant imperial power destined for judgment.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. While the phrase 'great city of the sea' is geographically anomalous, the core symbolic identification perfectly matches the primary consensus derived from Matthew 12.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book selects the primary consensus strand based on Jonah and Matthew 12 (repentant Gentiles judging the unrepentant) while narrowing out the secondary consensus strand based on Nahum (an oppressive empire judged by God).\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The book's description of Nineveh as a 'great city of the sea' is geographically inaccurate and absent from the consensus, and the entry entirely ignores the prominent prophetic tradition of Nineveh's destruction as a wicked empire.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
